  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Expressions and Equations Involving Square Roots",
    "description": "Use square-root relationships and identities to simplify expressions and solve unknowns.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the idea, derive the method, practice transfer, and use targeted remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5 “Square Roots and Cube Roots,” printed pages 180–205. Approved scope: Teach solving equations with square roots by isolating the root, squaring only after recording sign/domain restrictions, and checking candidate solutions in the original equation. Cover relationships among √x, x, and known perfect squares; expressions built from known roots; and chapter-supported identities such as (√a±√b)² and conjugate products. Explain principal-root non-negativity and reject extraneous results introduced by squaring. Include numerical and simple variable examples at the source level, not a general radical-equation unit. Learners should choose a reversible step, state constraints, simplify exactly, and substitute back."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Equations involving square roots require domain checks. Isolate the principal root, square carefully, and substitute every candidate into the original equation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The principal square root is non-negative. In √(x+9)=5, square both non-negative sides to get x+9=25, so x=16. Check in the original: √25=5. The check confirms both the algebra and the radicand condition.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
